mount.description:
{set}

A car carries one set of four, **or** two pairs — never a mix:

- a set of four takes both axles;
- fitting a pair takes off the whole set of four that was there — half a set of four is not a set of four.

On the car today — {fitted}.

retire.description:
{set}

This total is frozen and the set filed in the history: it can no longer be fitted.

Nothing is deleted — you can put it back in service, or delete the set if you do not care for its past.

delete.description:
{set}

The set leaves the list, with its sensor and its device.

The mileage itself is kept: adding the set again later finds it.

tpms.description:
{set}

One sensor per tyre — {slots}.

The sensor is screwed to the wheel: it belongs to the set and follows it from one car to the next. Temperature and battery are read from the same device, no need to name them. Clearing a field detaches the sensor.

rotate.description:
{set}

**{since} km** since the last rotation.

Each wheel moves to the other end of its side. The mileage does not change — the set has covered what it has covered — but the reminder starts again from here, and the pressure sensors follow their wheel.

separate.description:
{set}

It becomes two sets of two, each starting from that same total: those kilometres were covered on all four tyres, and they belong to both halves. From here on each pair counts for itself.

Nothing is taken off the car, and nothing is deleted.
